v 3
d EquiPop 1.53.0 - k-nearest neighbour context variables
d
d equipop builds, for every observation, the neighbourhood of its k
d nearest neighbours measured in people, and reports what that
d neighbourhood contains.
d
d Requires: Stata 17 or later, and Python with the equipop package
d installed in the Python that Stata uses. See help equipop.
d
d NOTE: Stata and Anaconda do not mix - Anaconda's numpy closes
d Stata on import. Use a plain python.org Python for Stata.
d
d Author: John Östh, OsloMet
d Support: https://github.com/GeoJohnSwe/EquiPop
d
d Distribution-Date: 20261006
f equipop.ado
f equipop.sthlp
f equipop_knn.ado
f equipop_run.ado
* BACKLOG 330. The worked examples and the test data were NEVER
* declared here, so `net install` from GitHub gave the commands and
* not the material the help and the paper tell people to run. The
* SSC archive builds its own .pkg and Kit Baum included them, which
* is how the two routes came to differ.
*
* BACKLOG 334, AND 330 GOT THIS WRONG FOR ONE RELEASE. These three
* lines said `g`, on the belief that `g` was the ancillary keyword.
* IT IS NOT: `g` is the PLATFORM-SPECIFIC directive, and its syntax
* is `g PLATFORMNAME sourcefile targetfile` - for shipping a
* different compiled plugin to WIN64A, MACARM64, LINUX64 and so on.
* So Stata would have read "equipop_example.do" as a platform name
* with no files after it, and these three would not have installed
* at all. Found by Marina's pull request (GitHub lizardie), which
* had them as `f` all along.
*
* `f` IS CORRECT FOR AN ANCILLARY FILE. Stata decides what to do with
* a file by its EXTENSION, not by a directive: .ado and .sthlp are
* installed into the ado path, while .do and .dta are ancillary and
* come down with `net get` - which is what the `all` option of
* `ssc install` and `net install` performs alongside the install.
f equipop_example.do
f equipop_showcase.do
f equipop_test_data.dta
